NAME
       logger - write a message to the system log

SYNOPSIS
       logger [-p FACILITY.LEVEL] [-t TAG] [-i] [-s] [MESSAGE...]
       logger [-p ...] [-t TAG] -f FILE

DESCRIPTION
       logger sends MESSAGE (or the lines of standard input or of a file) to
       the system log, as if a program had logged it. It reaches the journal
       (journalctl) and, through rsyslog, the text files in /var/log. Scripts
       and cron jobs use it to leave a note of what they did, and it is handy
       to test where log messages end up.

       Every syslog message has a facility (what kind of program sent it) and
       a level (how serious it is). rsyslog's rules in /etc/rsyslog.conf and
       /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf decide which file each combination goes to;
       for example authpriv.* goes to /var/log/secure, and most others of
       level info and above to /var/log/messages.

       Facilities: kern, user (the default), mail, daemon, auth, authpriv,
       syslog, cron, local0 to local7.
       Levels: emerg, alert, crit, err, warning, notice (the default), info,
       debug.

OPTIONS
       -p FACILITY.LEVEL, --priority FACILITY.LEVEL
              The facility and level, such as user.err or local3.info. A
              level alone (-p err) uses facility user.

       -t TAG, --tag TAG
              Put TAG in front of the message instead of your user name. Find
              the messages again with journalctl -t TAG.

       -i, --id
              Add logger's process ID.

       -s, --stderr
              Also print the message on standard error.

       -f FILE, --file FILE
              Log every line of FILE.

EXAMPLES
       Leave a note from a backup script:

           logger -t backup "backup of /etc finished"
           journalctl -t backup

       Log a test error and see it in the text log:

           logger -p user.err "Cert Lab test message"
           tail -n 3 /var/log/messages

       Test a custom rsyslog rule that sends local5 to its own file:

           echo "local5.* /var/log/local5.log" > /etc/rsyslog.d/local5.conf
           systemctl restart rsyslog
           logger -p local5.info "hello local5"
           cat /var/log/local5.log

SEE ALSO
       journalctl(1), rsyslogd(8), rsyslog.conf(5), systemd-cat(1)
